# The state lives in its own git repository. It stopped being ephemeral - the
# interaction clock, the read cursors and the inbox are records of what you have
# done and none of them can be re-derived - so it is worth a history, but not
# this one: mixed in here it buried the diffs that matter in the diffs that do
# not, and dirtied the tree on every refresh. `WORKLOG_DATA` points elsewhere.
data/

# A clone of Term.jl, kept here because this directory is the only one that
# survives the sandbox. It is somewhere to fix the three `TermMarkdown` bugs
# this program works around - `Term.jl/fixme.md` is the write-up of them, and
# is excluded from that checkout too so it cannot end up in a pull request.
/Term.jl/

# The two halves that were split out are **submodules** and not ignored - see
# `.gitmodules`. `cli/Project.toml` reaches them through `[sources]`, so a
# checkout of each has to be here for the program to build at all, and ignoring
# them meant a fresh clone of this repository could not build it. Their
# histories are still not this one's; a submodule is how a checkout carries a
# commit of somebody else's history without adopting it, which is exactly the
# arrangement. `[compat]` against a registered version is what this becomes
# once they are registered, and then they stop being directories here.
#
# `TermIFrame` is the tmux half: sessions, the control-mode client, and the box
# a hosted program is drawn in. `TermInput` is the composer, the line prompt,
# the key vocabulary they bind, and the escape-aware measuring under all of it -
# which `TermIFrame` depends on for the same measuring, since a text field must
# not pull a tmux binary in to measure a string.
#
# `Term.jl/` above stays ignored, deliberately: it is a clone of somebody
# else's package kept here to fix bugs in, not something this program builds
# against - `Term` comes from the registry - so there is no commit of it this
# repository has any business pinning.

# Vim's undo files, which it drops beside whatever is being edited. Only
# `config.toml` and the theme files are hand-edited here often enough for one to
# appear, but the pattern is the editor's and not those files'.
*.un~
